#%PAM-1.0
# sill's own lock screen: the password check `sill auth-check` runs.
# Installed as /etc/pam.d/sill by dist/install.sh; without it sill falls back to the `login`
# service. The same shape as swaylock's own file: the login stack decides which modules run.
auth     include  login
account  include  login
# The wallet, the way Plasma's own stacks have it (/usr/lib/pam.d/plasmalogin): the leading `-`
# makes a missing module no error, and `optional` means it can never fail an unlock. These two
# lines do not open the wallet at login: the display manager's PAM stack does that (its session
# module starts ksecretd), and dist/sill-session sends the password on to it. They are here so a
# login that goes through this service (a greeter pointed at it) behaves like Plasma's.
-auth    optional pam_kwallet5.so
-session optional pam_kwallet5.so auto_start
